  realLife: [
    { tag: 'HUMILITY', tagZh: '谦卑', verseRef: 'Proverbs 25:6',
      titleZh: '别急着为自己抢位置', titleEn: 'Don’t rush to claim your seat',
      bodyZh: '箴言 25:6–7 描绘了一个很有画面感的场景：宴席上，有人急着坐上高位，结果被主人当众请到较低的座位，尴尬难堪；而那甘居低位的人，反倒被请上来，得着真正的尊荣。耶稣后来用这幅画教导门徒（路加福音 14 章）。这在今天依然适用：在会议里抢功、在群体中自抬身价、急着让人知道“我很重要”——这些自己争来的地位，往往脆弱又易碎。智慧是甘心站在较低的地方，把抬举交给神和别人。',
      bodyEn: 'Proverbs 25:6–7 paints a vivid scene: at a banquet someone rushes to take the high seat, only to be publicly moved down by the host — humiliating; while the one who gladly took the lower place is invited up to real honor. Jesus later used this picture with his disciples (Luke 14). It still applies: grabbing credit in a meeting, inflating your status in a group, rushing to make sure everyone knows “I matter” — status you seize for yourself tends to be fragile and easily lost. Wisdom gladly takes the lower place and leaves the lifting up to God and others.' },
    { tag: 'WORDS', tagZh: '言语', verseRef: 'Proverbs 25:11',
      titleZh: '对的话，还要说在对的时候', titleEn: 'The right word, at the right time',
      bodyEn: 'A word fitly spoken is like apples of gold in settings of silver (25:11). Notice the emphasis isn’t only on saying the right thing but on timing — the word “fitly,” at the right moment, in the right way. We’ve all had true words land badly because they came at the wrong time or in the wrong tone; and we’ve all felt the deep refreshment of an encouragement that arrived exactly when we needed it. Wisdom pays attention not just to what is true but to when and how to say it. Sometimes the most loving thing is to wait for the right moment.',
      bodyZh: '“一句话说得合宜，就如金苹果在银网子里”（25:11）。请注意，重点不只在于说对的话，更在于时机——那个“合宜”，是在对的时刻、以对的方式说出来。我们都经历过：一句本来是真话，却因为说错了时候、用错了语气，反而伤了人；我们也都体会过，一句恰在我们最需要时送到的鼓励，带来何等深的滋润。智慧不只关注内容是否正确，也关注何时说、怎么说。有时候，最有爱心的做法，是等一个对的时机。' },
    { tag: 'ENEMIES', tagZh: '仇敌', verseRef: 'Proverbs 25:21',
      titleZh: '当你有机会报复', titleEn: 'When you have the chance to get even',
      bodyZh: '“你的仇敌若饿了，就给他饭吃；若渴了，就给他水喝”（25:21）。这句话违反我们最深的本能——当那个伤过我们的人落难时，心里第一反应往往是暗自称快，甚至想补上一脚。但圣经指向一条更高的路：以善待代替报复。这不是假装没受伤，也不是纵容不义，而是把伸冤交给神（参 25:22；罗马书 12 章），自己选择做那件具体的善事。这样行，既可能软化对方的心，也保守了我们自己不被苦毒吞噬。',
      bodyEn: 'If your enemy is hungry, give him food; if he is thirsty, give him water (25:21). This cuts against our deepest instinct — when the person who hurt us stumbles, our first reaction is often quiet satisfaction, even the urge to pile on. But Scripture points to a higher road: replace revenge with kindness. This isn’t pretending you weren’t hurt, nor excusing injustice; it’s handing vindication to God (see 25:22; Romans 12) and choosing the concrete good deed yourself. Doing so may soften the other person’s heart — and it guards your own from being consumed by bitterness.' },
    { tag: 'SELF-CONTROL', tagZh: '自制', verseRef: 'Proverbs 25:28',
      titleZh: '没有城墙的城', titleEn: 'A city with no walls',
      bodyZh: '“人不制伏自己的心，好像毁坏的城邑没有墙垣”（25:28）。在古代，城墙就是一座城的生死线；墙一旦破了，再繁华的城也任凭外敌长驱直入。这幅比喻精准地道出自制的重要：一个人可以极有才干、极有恩赐，但若管不住自己的脾气、舌头、消费或欲望，这些破口就会让他前功尽弃。好消息是，城墙可以一块一块地重建。先找出你最明显的那个破口，今天就为它砌上第一块砖——设一个界限、道一次歉、或停下一个冲动。',
      bodyEn: 'Like a city broken down, without walls, is a person whose spirit is without restraint (25:28). In the ancient world, the wall was a city’s lifeline; once it was breached, even a prosperous city lay open to any invader. The image captures why self-control matters so much: a person can be enormously talented and gifted, yet if they can’t govern their temper, tongue, spending, or appetites, those breaches can undo everything. The good news is that walls can be rebuilt, one stone at a time. Find your most obvious breach and lay the first brick today — set a boundary, offer an apology, or stop one impulse.' },
  ],

